  "Check, Call, Care. Check that the scene is safe, then check the person. Call 911 (or send someone) when it is serious. Care for the person until help takes over.": "qwen3-tts-1.7b-base clone",
  "When to call 911. Someone isn't responding or isn't breathing normally, can't breathe, has heavy bleeding that won't stop, has signs of heat stroke, has a seizure longer than 5 minutes, or you're not sure. Put the phone on speaker so your hands are free.": "qwen3-tts-1.7b-base clone",
  "Hands-only CPR. For a teen or adult who suddenly collapses: call 911, then push hard and fast in the center of the chest, 100 to 120 a minute, at least 2 inches deep, letting the chest come all the way up. Don't stop until help takes over. For infants and young children, a class teaches CPR with breaths.": "qwen3-tts-1.7b-base clone",
  "AEDs. An AED is a portable device that can check the heart's rhythm and give a shock if needed. Turn it on and follow its voice prompts; it won't shock someone who doesn't need it. Many schools and gyms have one: find yours.": "qwen3-tts-1.7b-base clone",
  "Good to know. A class with a manikin teaches depth, hand position and how it feels. The American Heart Association and the American Red Cross offer them, and many schools teach hands-only CPR.": "qwen3-tts-1.7b-base clone"
}
